    externalId: "policy-access-control-policy",
    title: "Access Control Policy",
    sourceType: "policy",
    content: "Access Control Policy\nSolsphere AI Inc. | Regodit\n1. Purpose and Scope\nThis Access Control Policy establishes how access to the company’s information systems, applications, and data is granted, managed, and revoked, following the principles of least privilege and need-to-know. It applies to all founders, employees, interns, contractors, and third parties, and to all logical access to the company’s systems, cloud platforms, and applications.\nThe policy is proportionate to a small, cloud-native, remote-first team and works alongside the Information Security Policy, the Password/Secrets Policy, the Human Resource Policy, the Data Classification Policy, and the Incident Management Policy.\n2. Roles and Responsibilities\nThe Chief Executive Officer, acting as Chief Information Security Officer, owns this policy and the access model, and leads the annual access review.\nThe Chief Technology Officer administers access provisioning, changes, and revocation across systems, holds standing production access, and approves production-access grants.\nThe Chief Business Officer / Chief Privacy Officer owns non-technical access matters and approves access to business and people systems.\nAll users use only authorized credentials, protect them, and report any suspicious activity or unauthorized access.\n3. User Access Provisioning\nNew access requests are approved by the relevant lead and granted on a least-privilege basis according to role. Provisioning is executed by the designated administrator. Access granted at onboarding is aligned with the onboarding process defined in the Human Resource Policy.\n4. Authentication\nMulti-factor authentication is enforced across all core systems, including cloud infrastructure consoles, the identity and email provider, and the source-code platform. Password construction, rotation, and storage requirements are defined in the Password/Secrets Policy.\n5. Privileged and Production Access\nStanding production access is limited to the Chief Technology Officer. Other personnel obtain production access from the Chief Technology Officer on a need basis, granted for the duration required. Any production change requires approval from the Chief Technology Officer, the Chief Business Officer / Chief Privacy Officer, or the Chief Executive Officer. Privileged actions are logged.\n6. Inactive Accounts\nAccounts that have been inactive for 90 days are disabled. This is currently performed manually, with automation planned.\n7. Access Reviews\nAccess to all systems is reviewed at least annually across all users to confirm that privileges remain appropriate to each person’s role. Findings are documented and remediated, and unnecessary access is removed.\n8. Separation of Duties\nSegregation of duties is applied where feasible; the company acknowledges that team size limits full separation. Rather than enforcing a strict separation between development and production, the company restricts standing production access to the Chief Technology Officer, logs production activity, and gates production changes through the approval requirement in Section 5.\n9. Remote Access\nRemote access to internal resources is being consolidated onto the company VPN, protected by multi-factor authentication. Endpoints used for remote access must meet the company’s security requirements, and sessions time out after a period of inactivity.\n10. Third-Party and Vendor Access\nVendors and contractors receive access only on the basis of business necessity and least privilege. Contracts include access-control and security obligations, and vendor access is included in the annual access review. Vendor risk is further governed by the Risk Management Policy.\n11. Access Revocation and Offboarding\nOn termination or offboarding, access is revoked promptly. Revocation is requested through the company’s internal channel and executed by the designated administrator, with the Chief Technology Officer accountable for completion, aligned with the offboarding checklist in the Human Resource Policy. Temporary access permissions expire at their defined end.\n12. Logging and Monitoring of Access\nAccess and privileged activity are logged using cloud-native logging. The cloud provider’s logging service retains logs for approximately one year, and logs shipped to object storage are retained on a long-term basis. Logs are reviewed for anomalies and unauthorized access attempts.\n13. Integration with Other Policies\nThis policy implements, in operational detail, the identity and authorization requirements stated in the Information Security Policy. It works most closely with the Password and Secrets Policy, which governs the credentials, secrets, and authentication factors that this policy relies on to establish identity; the two are intended to be read together.\nWhat a person may reach is determined by the sensitivity of the data involved, as defined by the Data Classification Policy, and applied to the systems and devices recorded under the Asset Management Policy. Access to cryptographic keys and secrets is granted under this policy and protected as set out in the Cryptography Policy. Provisioning at onboarding and revocation at departure are steps within the processes defined in the Human Resource Policy, and the conduct expected of an account holder is set out in the Code of Conduct Policy.\nUnauthorized access, whether suspected or confirmed, is handled as an event under the Incident Management Policy, and access-related exposures that cannot be immediately resolved are recorded and treated under the Risk Management Policy. The approval requirement for production changes stated here is also relied on by the Vulnerability and Patch Management Policy and by the Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery Policy.\n14. Compliance and Review\nAccess-control practices support the company’s SOC 2 objectives. This policy is reviewed at least annually and updated as the company grows or its controls change.\n15. Policy Acknowledgment\nAll personnel are required to ac",
    author: "Solsphere AI Inc. (Regodit)",
    scope: "workforce and privileged access",
    sourceDate: "2026-08-01",
