    externalId: "policy-asset-management-policy",
    title: "Asset Management Policy",
    sourceType: "policy",
    content: "Asset Management Policy\nSolsphere AI Inc.| Regodit\n1. Purpose and Scope\nThis Asset Management Policy establishes how the company identifies, records, protects, maintains, and disposes of the assets used to run its business and deliver its product. It applies to all founders, employees, interns, contractors, and third parties who use or manage company assets.\nIn scope are endpoint devices used for company work, cloud infrastructure resources, software-as-a-service subscriptions and applications, source code and data, and the credentials that grant access to them. The company operates no on-premises servers, data centers, or server rooms; physical and environmental controls for production infrastructure are provided by the cloud providers. This policy works alongside the Information Security Policy, the Access Control Policy, the Data Classification Policy, the Cryptography Policy, and the Vulnerability and Patch Management Policy.\n2. Roles and Responsibilities\nThe Chief Executive Officer, acting as Chief Information Security Officer, owns this policy, owns cloud infrastructure and IT assets, and leads the annual asset review.\nThe Chief Technology Officer owns source code, deployment tooling, and production resources, and ensures security configuration is applied to assets before use.\nThe Chief Business Officer / Chief Privacy Officer owns non-technical assets, including business and people-operations subscriptions, and coordinates device issue and return with onboarding and offboarding.\nAll personnel use company assets in line with this policy, protect the assets in their possession, and report loss, theft, or malfunction promptly.\n3. Asset Inventory\nThe company maintains its asset inventory on its internal compliance platform, which serves as the single record of assets in use.\nEndpoint devices used for company work are recorded on the internal compliance platform, including the assigned holder and whether the device is company-issued or personally owned.\nCloud infrastructure resources are collected automatically on a daily basis and maintained on the internal compliance platform, so the infrastructure inventory reflects the current state of the environment rather than a periodic manual snapshot.\nSoftware-as-a-service subscriptions and applications holding company or customer data are recorded, together with their business owner.\nInventory records identify the asset, its owner or assigned holder, and its criticality. The inventory is reviewed at least annually alongside the access review described in the Access Control Policy, and is updated on issue, return, or decommissioning of an asset.\n4. Asset Classification\nAssets are classified according to the sensitivity of the data they hold or access, using the classification levels defined in the Data Classification Policy. Assets that store or process Confidential or Restricted data are subject to the strongest protection requirements, including encryption and restricted access.\n5. Endpoint Security\nBoth company-issued and personally owned devices may be used for company work. Company-issued devices are the default for personnel in roles that require them. Security requirements attach to the device and the data on it rather than to its ownership, and apply equally in both cases.\nEvery device used for company work must have full-disk encryption enabled, must run a supported operating system kept current with security updates, must use the operating system’s native malware protection and firewall, and must be protected by a screen lock and strong authentication. Devices are hardened using the operating system’s native security configuration. The company does not deploy a separate commercial endpoint detection and response product; it relies on operating-system native protections, which are verified as part of the annual asset review.\nLoss or theft of any device used for company work must be reported immediately and is handled under the Incident Management Policy, including revocation of the credentials held on that device.\n6. Cloud and Production Assets\nProduction and non-production workloads run in separate cloud accounts. Network access to production resources is controlled using cloud-native firewalls configured to restrict inbound traffic. Production data stores are encrypted at rest, and customer data used outside production is anonymized or protected to the same standard as production data, as set out in the Cryptography Policy and the Data Classification Policy.\nAccess to production resources is restricted and logged, as defined in the Access Control Policy.\n7. Asset Lifecycle\nAcquisition and Deployment\nAssets are procured through authorized channels and recorded on the internal compliance platform before or at issue. Security configuration, including disk encryption, operating-system updates, and access provisioning on a least-privilege basis, is applied before an asset is used for company work.\nMaintenance\nAssets are kept on supported software versions and patched in line with the Vulnerability and Patch Management Policy. Inventory records are updated as assets are issued, reassigned, or retired.\nReturn and Disposal\nCompany-issued devices are collected before a person’s departure and are securely wiped before reissue or disposal. Where a personally owned device has been used for company work, company data is removed and access is revoked at departure. Device return and data removal are carried out as part of the offboarding process defined in the Human Resource Policy, alongside the access revocation described in the Access Control Policy, and the inventory record is updated to reflect the outcome.\nCloud storage and infrastructure are decommissioned using the cloud provider’s deletion mechanisms, with cryptographic erasure of associated keys as described in the Cryptography Policy. Physical destruction of media in the cloud environment is the responsibility of the cloud provider under its own certified processes.\n8. Acceptable Use\nC",
