    externalId: "policy-incident-management-policy",
    title: "Incident Management Policy",
    sourceType: "policy",
    content: "Incident Management Policy\nSolsphere AI Inc. | Regodit\n1. Purpose\nThis policy establishes the framework by which Solsphere identifies, classifies, responds to, communicates about, and learns from information security and availability incidents. It is designed to ensure a consistent, timely, and effective response that limits impact to Solsphere, its clients, and its data, and to satisfy the incident management requirements of SOC 2 (Security and Availability Trust Services Criteria)\n2. Scope\nThis policy applies to all incidents that affect the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of Solsphere systems, data, or the systems/data of clients processed on Solsphere's behalf. It applies to all employees, contractors, and third parties with access to Solsphere or client environments.\n3. Definitions\nEvent: Any observable occurrence in a system or network (e.g., a login, a failed process, a firewall alert).\nIncident: An event, or series of events, that compromises the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of information or systems, or that violates policy.\nBreach: An incident confirmed to have resulted in unauthorized access to, acquisition of, or disclosure of confidential, personal, or regulated data.\nIncident Response Team (IRT): The designated group of individuals responsible for managing the response to an incident.\n4. Roles and Responsibilities\nIRT Lead / Information Security Officer: Owns the incident end-to-end, coordinates the response, and has authority to declare an incident and its severity. Also manages internal updates and drafts external/client/regulator communications for approval.\nSenior Management: Informed for SEV-1/SEV-2 incidents; approves external communications and any regulatory filings.\n5. Incident Classification\nIncidents are classified by severity to ensure response effort and escalation are proportionate to impact. The criteria and response times below are illustrative starting points and must be tuned to Solsphere's actual SLAs and client commitments.\n6. Detection and Reporting\nIncidents may be identified through automated monitoring and alerting, employee or contractor reports, client reports, or third-party notifications (e.g., vendor breach disclosures).\nAll employees and contractors must report a suspected incident to the IRT lead as soon as possible and no later than 1 hour after discovery.\nThe IRT Lead validates the report, opens a formal incident record, and assigns an initial severity classification within 30 minutes of report.\nAn incident log (timeline, actions taken, decisions, evidence references) is maintained for every SEV-0/SEV-1 incident.\n7. Incident Response Lifecycle\nOnce declared, an incident proceeds through the following phases. Phases may overlap or iterate depending on incident complexity.\n8. Communication Plan\nInternal communication: The IRT Lead provides status updates to the Senior Management and relevant stakeholders at a cadence appropriate to severity\nClient communication: Where an incident affects a client's data or service, Solsphere will notify the client in accordance with the notification timeline and method specified in the applicable client contract/DPA.\nRegulatory notification: Where an incident constitutes a reportable breach of personal data, Solsphere will assess and comply with applicable notification obligations.\nSpokesperson: All external and media communications are approved by and issued through Senior Management (CEO, CBO) only\n9. Evidence Handling and Chain of Custody\nRelevant logs, system images, and artifacts must be preserved in their original state as soon as an incident is declared, before any remediation that could alter or destroy evidence.\nAccess to preserved evidence is restricted to the IRT and logged.\nWhere law enforcement or legal action is reasonably anticipated, evidence handling follows chain-of-custody practices sufficient to preserve admissibility.\n10. Post-Incident Review\nWithin 5 business days of resolution of any SEV-0 or SEV-1 incident, the IRT conducts a post-incident review to document the root cause, full timeline, effectiveness of the response, and corrective actions. Corrective actions are tracked to closure and, where they indicate a gap in controls, fed back into the risk register and risk treatment plan.\n11. Testing and Training\nAll employees receive security awareness training, including how to recognize and report a suspected incident at onboarding and annually thereafter\nLessons learned from tabletop exercises and real incidents are used to update this policy and associated playbooks.\n12. Metrics and Reporting\nThe IRT Lead tracks and reports incident metrics, incident volume, and severity trends as part of periodic management review meetings\n13. Related Documents\nBusiness Continuity Policy\nRisk Management Policy\nAccess Control Policy\n14. Exceptions\nAny exception to this policy must be documented, justified on a risk basis, and approved in writing by the CISO/Security Lead, with a defined review date.\n15. Policy Enforcement\nViolation of this policy may result in disciplinary action up to and including termination of employment or contract, consistent with Solsphere's HR policies and applicable law.\n16. Document Control\nRevision History\nApproval\nDocument Title | Incident Management Policy\nDocument Owner | [MODIFY: Insert owner name/role]\nApproved By | [MODIFY: Insert approver name/role]\nClassification | Internal / Confidential\nEffective Date\nReview Cycle | Annual, or upon material change to systems, regulations, or organizational structure\nApplies To | All employees, contractors, and third parties with access to company or client systems/data\nSeverity | Criteria (example) | Initial Response Time | Escalation\nSEV-0 (Critical) | Confirmed data breach involving client or regulated data; full service outage; active exploitation | 1 hour | Immediate notification to IRT lead and Senior Management\nSEV-1 (High) | Significant degradation of a core service; suspected but unconfirmed breach;",
    author: "Solsphere AI Inc. (Regodit)",
    scope: "security incident response",
    sourceDate: "2026-08-01",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-01",
    reliability: "high",
