    externalId: "policy-information-security-policy",
    title: "Information Security Policy",
    sourceType: "policy",
    content: "Information Security Policy\nSolsphere AI Inc. | Regodit\n1. Purpose and Scope\nThis Information Security Policy defines how the company protects the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of the information it holds and processes. It applies to all information, data, cloud environments, endpoints, and SaaS applications owned, operated, or managed by the company, and to all founders, employees, interns, contractors, and third parties with access to those systems or data.\nThe policy is designed to be proportionate to the size and stage of the organization, a small, cloud-native, remote-first team, rather than modeled on enterprise-scale frameworks. Controls are scoped to what the company implements and can evidence today, with planned improvements noted where relevant. It works alongside the Access Control, Data Classification, Cryptography, Vulnerability and Patch Management, Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery, Risk Management, Incident Management, and Human Resource policies.\n2. Roles and Responsibilities\nGiven the company’s size, it does not maintain separate security, incident-response, disaster-recovery, or internal-audit teams. These functions are performed by the founders.\nThe Chief Executive Officer, acting as Chief Information Security Officer, holds overall accountability for information security, owns cloud infrastructure and IT, approves policies, and leads the annual risk assessment.\nThe Chief Technology Officer owns application code, deployments, and change management, is primarily responsible for access provisioning and deprovisioning, and holds standing production access.\nThe Chief Business Officer / Chief Privacy Officer is responsible for all non-technical functions, including people operations, privacy, and compliance.\nAll personnel comply with policies, report suspected incidents, and complete security awareness training.\nThird parties and vendors adhere to the security and confidentiality obligations in their contracts.\nSegregation of duties is applied where feasible; the company acknowledges that team size limits full separation of some duties.\n3. Risk Management\nA risk assessment is conducted at least annually, led by the acting CISO, and is supplemented by third-party vulnerability assessment and penetration testing. Identified risks are tracked to remediation on a risk-based schedule. The overall approach is governed by the Risk Management Policy.\n4. Information Classification and Handling\nInformation is classified as Public, Internal, Confidential, or Restricted. The Data Classification Policy is the single source of truth for these levels and for the handling, retention, and disposal requirements attached to each. Confidential and Restricted information is access-controlled and encrypted; Public information is managed for accuracy but is not access-restricted.\n5. Access Control and Authentication\nAccess follows least privilege and is granted based on role. Multi-factor authentication is required across all core systems, including cloud infrastructure consoles, the identity and email provider, and the source-code platform. Production and non-production workloads run in separate cloud accounts. Detailed provisioning, review, and revocation controls are defined in the Access Control Policy.\n6. Personnel Security\nBackground checks are conducted for all personnel through a third-party verification provider. Non-disclosure obligations are included in offer letters for all personnel, and any missing agreements are backfilled. Onboarding includes provisioning of least-privilege access and security awareness training. On offboarding, access is revoked promptly: revocation is requested through the company’s internal channel and executed by the designated administrator, with the Chief Technology Officer accountable for completion. Personnel controls are governed by the Human Resource Policy.\n7. Security Awareness Training\nSecurity awareness training is delivered through the company’s own platform, at onboarding and on an ongoing, periodic basis. This program has recently been instituted and will continue on a regular cadence.\n8. Network and Infrastructure Security\nNetwork access is controlled using cloud-native firewalls configured to restrict inbound traffic. Production and non-production environments are separated at the cloud account level. Remote access to internal resources is being consolidated onto the company VPN; this rollout is in progress. The company operates no on-premises servers or data centers.\n9. Endpoint Security\nEndpoints used for work are kept current with operating-system security updates and built-in protections. Disk encryption is enabled where supported by the device. Devices accessing company systems must meet basic security requirements.\n10. Cryptography\nSensitive data is encrypted at rest using AES-256 and in transit using TLS. Cryptographic standards and key handling are defined in the Cryptography Policy.\n11. Logging and Monitoring\nCloud-native monitoring is used across the company’s cloud providers. Application logs are rotated on the host and shipped to object storage for retention. The cloud provider’s logging service retains logs for approximately one year, and object storage is retained on a long-term basis. The company does not currently operate a dedicated SIEM; centralized log analytics is a planned improvement.\n12. Remote Work and Physical Security\nThe workforce is remote-first. Encrypted collaboration and email are provided through the company’s productivity suite, and endpoints are kept updated. VPN access is being rolled out for internal resources. Production systems are hosted with major cloud providers, which provide physical and environmental security for their data centers; the company owns no data centers or server rooms. Physical office presence is limited to a co-working facility with card-based building access, and most staff work remotely.\n13. Change Management and Secure Development\nSource code is managed in a ve",
    author: "Solsphere AI Inc. (Regodit)",
    scope: "company-wide information security program",
    sourceDate: "2026-08-01",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-01",
    reliability: "high",
